+def compute_policy_staleness_metrics(
+    log_prob: torch.Tensor, old_log_prob: torch.Tensor, loss_mask: torch.Tensor
+) -> dict[str, Metric]:
+    """How far the policy has moved from the one that generated these actions.
+
+    Replay trains on responses sampled under ``old_log_prob``, so the further the
+    current policy has walked away the less the objective's proximal anchor
+    means. Adaptive replay uses these to decide when to stop reusing a batch.
+
+    Two views, because they fail differently. The mean square log-ratio is the
+    typical per-token drift and grows smoothly. The effective sample size
+    fraction is the share of the batch the importance weights still represent,
+    and it falls off a cliff once a few tokens dominate the weights.
+
+    Both are worth stating plainly: the PPO ratio and its clipping only correct
+    the *action* mismatch on tokens that were actually observed. Neither can
+    repair the state-distribution mismatch of a stale trajectory, whose prefixes
+    the current policy would no longer visit. So these are a signal to stop
+    replaying in time, not a licence to keep old data forever.
